 "_about": "SERIES SHELF of Philosophical Classics for English Readers (Edinburgh and London: William Blackwood and Sons, 1880-1890; American issue Philadelphia: J. B. Lippincott), edited by William Knight: one book per great philosopher, life and thought explained in English by a British scholar. Fourteen volumes, each dated on its title page (most in Roman numerals; a few are early Blackwood reprints, dated with their first year): Descartes (J. P. Mahaffy, 1880), Butler (W. Lucas Collins, 1881), Berkeley (Alexander Campbell Fraser, 1881, reprint 1884), Fichte (Robert Adamson, 1881), Kant (William Wallace, 1882), Hegel (Edward Caird, 1883, reprint 1886), Leibniz (John Theodore Merz, 1884; the OCR reads the year MDCCCLXXX1V), Vico (Robert Flint, 1884), Hobbes (George Croom Robertson, 1886), Hume (William Knight, 1886), Spinoza (John Caird, 1888, reprint 1899), Francis Bacon in two parts (John Nichol, 1888-1889) and Locke (Alexander Campbell Fraser, 1890). Raw Internet Archive OCR, clean 0.90-0.95. The fifteenth, John Veitch's Hamilton (1882), is already on the john-veitch shelf and is not repeated. Not used: a 1969 Books for Libraries reprint of Mahaffy's Descartes, and scans of Wallace's Kant and Caird's Spinoza whose OCR lacks the author's name. Each title gates on its own author's name. US public domain. No uids minted.",
